{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cg6bL1bpCSxyF7gBr7PCE", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "```\nTable 3 reports the estimates, and the pattern is clear: who writes a review matters, not just what it says. In column 2, the coefficient on the identity-disclosed indicator is 0.18 (SE = 0.04). It remains substantial in column 3 after we add product fixed effects, at 0.15 (SE = 0.05). The effect also grows with experience: the interaction with reviewer tenure is 0.09 (SE = 0.03) in column 4. Star rating, as expected, carries a strong effect of its own, with a coefficient of 0.42 (SE = 0.02) across all specifications. Model fit improves accordingly, as R-squared rises from 0.31 to 0.47 across the columns. All coefficients are significant at the 1% level except the interaction, which is significant at the 5% level.\n```\n\nI opened with the thesis-level takeaway and used connective phrases (\"remains substantial,\" \"grows with experience\") to give the results a progression. Every number is unchanged. Two small interpretive additions are worth checking: \"as expected\" for the star rating, and \"improves accordingly\" for R-squared, which implies the added terms drive the fit gain. If you don't want either implication, I can remove them. Also, \"grows with experience\" reads the tenure interaction as a moderation effect, which the coefficient supports but you may prefer to word more cautiously."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1878, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1496, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1878}, "output_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "19b61